    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d118">

                <pb facs="0288" n="256" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v19/pg256"/>

                <head>118. Memorandum of Conversation<anchor n="0" xml:id="d118-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" type="conversation-or-meeting-date"
                            when="1958-09-22T08:45:00-04:00">September 22, 1958, 8:45
                        a.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <item>The Secretary</item>
                    <item>General Cabell</item>
                    <item>Mr. Bissell<anchor n="1" xml:id="d118-f2"/></item>
                </list>
                <p>General Cabell said that weather conditions were at the moment propitious for an
                    aerial inspection along the China coast designed to disclose particularly the
                    Communist naval dispositions and to confirm the presumed movement of troops from
                    the interior.</p>
                <p>I said I felt that in the light of the very tense situation and the possibility
                    that it might come up in the United Nations, we should not engage <pb
                        facs="0289" n="257" xml:id="pg257"/> in what admittedly would be a violation
                    of the air space of Communist China. I said that if he wished I would discuss
                    this with the President on Tuesday.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d118-f3"/></p>
                <p>General Cabell said he was not sure the weather would hold another day and that
                    he would therefore hold the request in abeyance pending what might be another
                    propitious day.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">JFD</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
